1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,177
So far, every valid tool call simply runs.

2
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:06,464
That's fine for listing tasks.

3
00:00:06,814 --> 00:00:11,732
It's not fine for deleting your
data, sending an email, or paying a bill.

4
00:00:12,082 --> 00:00:17,975
In this lesson you'll give every tool a
permission level, add a read-only mode, and

5
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:22,306
make the agent ask a human
before anything dangerous happens.

6
00:00:24,106 --> 00:00:27,565
You'll explain the principle
of least privilege for agents.

7
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:31,113
Sort tools into read, write, and danger.

8
00:00:31,713 --> 00:00:33,840
Block writes in a read-only mode.

9
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:37,546
And ask a human before
every dangerous action.

10
00:00:39,346 --> 00:00:44,577
Let's start with an idea security
engineers use everywhere: least privilege.

11
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:46,710
Think of a hotel key card.

12
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,438
It doesn't open
every door in the building.

13
00:00:49,788 --> 00:00:51,945
It opens your room, for your stay.

14
00:00:52,295 --> 00:00:54,378
If you lose it, the damage is small.

15
00:00:54,728 --> 00:01:00,795
For agents, least privilege means: give
the agent only the tools and powers it needs

16
00:01:00,795 --> 00:01:03,034
for this job, and nothing more.

17
00:01:03,634 --> 00:01:06,064
Why does this matter so much for agents?

18
00:01:06,414 --> 00:01:12,374
Because a model can misunderstand a
request, and a user, or text inside a document,

19
00:01:12,374 --> 00:01:15,892
can try to trick it
into doing something harmful.

20
00:01:16,242 --> 00:01:20,226
Permissions limit the damage,
even when the model gets it wrong.

21
00:01:22,026 --> 00:01:24,151
Our project uses three levels.

22
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:26,833
Read: list tasks and search notes.

23
00:01:27,183 --> 00:01:31,320
These are always allowed,
because looking never changes anything.

24
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,941
Write: add task,
complete task, and add note.

25
00:01:35,291 --> 00:01:41,104
These change data, so they're allowed,
unless the agent runs in read-only mode.

26
00:01:41,704 --> 00:01:43,545
And danger: delete task.

27
00:01:43,895 --> 00:01:48,759
Deleting can't be undone, so a
human must approve every single call.

28
00:01:50,559 --> 00:01:51,926
Here's the policy.

29
00:01:52,276 --> 00:01:58,874
It holds the context, a read-only flag,
and ask human: a function that asks a person

30
00:01:58,874 --> 00:02:00,523
a yes or no question.

31
00:02:00,873 --> 00:02:05,550
If there's nobody to ask, it's
None, and dangerous calls are denied.

32
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,504
It also keeps a log of every decision.

33
00:02:09,104 --> 00:02:11,329
The check runs before every call.

34
00:02:11,679 --> 00:02:14,581
Read tools return straight away: allowed.

35
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:21,255
In read-only mode, any other tool is
blocked, with a clear reason the model can pass

36
00:02:21,255 --> 00:02:22,339
on to the user.

37
00:02:22,939 --> 00:02:28,454
For danger tools, we build a clear
question, using the tool's confirm function.

38
00:02:28,804 --> 00:02:34,594
For delete task, it says which task, by
number and title, so the human knows exactly

39
00:02:34,594 --> 00:02:36,179
what they're approving.

40
00:02:36,779 --> 00:02:37,855
Then we ask.

41
00:02:38,205 --> 00:02:40,351
Anything other than yes means no.

42
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:46,466
The decision is logged, and if it's
declined, the model gets a tool error that says:

43
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:48,799
the user declined, don't retry it.

44
00:02:50,599 --> 00:02:52,200
Let's try four calls.

45
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,760
In read-only mode,
listing tasks is allowed.

46
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:59,949
But completing a task is
blocked, and nothing changes.

47
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:02,128
Now a dangerous call.

48
00:03:02,478 --> 00:03:06,364
The policy asks the
human: delete task one, pay rent?

49
00:03:06,714 --> 00:03:11,053
This human says no, so the call
is declined, and the task stays.

50
00:03:11,653 --> 00:03:16,141
And when the human says yes, only
then does the delete actually run.

51
00:03:17,941 --> 00:03:19,920
The order of these checks matters.

52
00:03:20,270 --> 00:03:22,021
First, does the tool exist?

53
00:03:22,371 --> 00:03:23,476
If not, error.

54
00:03:24,076 --> 00:03:25,862
Second, is the input valid?

55
00:03:26,212 --> 00:03:28,904
We never ask a human to approve nonsense.

56
00:03:29,504 --> 00:03:32,253
Third, for a delete, does the task exist?

57
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:34,642
We need it to write a clear question.

58
00:03:35,242 --> 00:03:40,135
Fourth, the permission check, which
asks the human only for danger tools.

59
00:03:40,735 --> 00:03:43,139
And fifth, run the tool with its timeout.

60
00:03:44,939 --> 00:03:46,587
Now in the real agent.

61
00:03:46,937 --> 00:03:50,418
An empty store, and two
tasks added, in one turn.

62
00:03:51,018 --> 00:03:52,393
Delete task one.

63
00:03:52,743 --> 00:03:54,527
The agent stops and asks.

64
00:03:54,877 --> 00:03:58,950
Here I pipe in the answer no,
so you can see it in the recording.

65
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:02,330
The delete is
declined, and the task is safe.

66
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:06,778
And in read-only mode,
even adding a task is blocked.

67
00:04:07,128 --> 00:04:11,958
That's a good default for an agent
that should only answer questions about your

68
00:04:11,958 --> 00:04:12,264
data.

69
00:04:14,064 --> 00:04:16,382
One warning about confirmations.

70
00:04:16,732 --> 00:04:23,124
A question like, allow delete task, task
ID one, teaches people to click yes without

71
00:04:23,124 --> 00:04:23,732
reading.

72
00:04:24,082 --> 00:04:30,114
A good question shows exactly what will
happen, in plain words: delete task one, pay

73
00:04:30,114 --> 00:04:31,550
rent, high priority.

74
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:37,583
Ask for every dangerous call, not just
the first, and treat no answer as a no.

75
00:04:39,383 --> 00:04:42,911
Pause and choose a permission
level for each of these tools.

76
00:04:43,511 --> 00:04:44,149
One.

77
00:04:44,499 --> 00:04:45,926
Get weather, for a city.

78
00:04:46,526 --> 00:04:47,147
Two.

79
00:04:47,497 --> 00:04:48,638
Rename task.

80
00:04:49,238 --> 00:04:49,935
Three.

81
00:04:50,285 --> 00:04:51,347
Send email.

82
00:04:57,947 --> 00:05:00,353
Get weather is read: it only looks.

83
00:05:00,703 --> 00:05:05,326
Rename task is write: it
changes data, but it's easy to undo.

84
00:05:05,676 --> 00:05:10,896
Send email is danger: once it's sent,
you can't take it back, so a human should

85
00:05:10,896 --> 00:05:12,086
approve every one.

86
00:05:13,886 --> 00:05:15,027
Interview corner.

87
00:05:15,377 --> 00:05:17,922
How do you make a tool-using agent safe?

88
00:05:20,522 --> 00:05:23,688
Least privilege, so
only the tools it needs.

89
00:05:24,038 --> 00:05:25,245
Permission levels.

90
00:05:25,595 --> 00:05:27,258
Validate every input.

91
00:05:27,608 --> 00:05:28,477
Timeouts.

92
00:05:28,827 --> 00:05:34,671
And human approval for actions that can't
be undone, with a clear confirmation and a

93
00:05:34,671 --> 00:05:36,202
log of every decision.

94
00:05:36,552 --> 00:05:40,296
The trap: saying, tell the
model in the prompt to be careful.

95
00:05:40,646 --> 00:05:42,151
Prompts guide the model.

96
00:05:42,501 --> 00:05:44,718
Only code enforces the rules.

97
00:05:45,318 --> 00:05:48,241
Follow-up: where should
the approval check live?

98
00:05:50,841 --> 00:05:56,782
In your code, between validation and
execution, so it runs no matter what the model

99
00:05:56,782 --> 00:05:57,140
says.

100
00:05:57,490 --> 00:06:02,390
You can tell the model about it, but
never rely on the model to enforce it.

101
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:07,451
The trap: asking the model to ask
the user for permission in its reply.

102
00:06:09,251 --> 00:06:10,110
Quick check.

103
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:14,896
Why does the policy validate
the input before asking the human?

104
00:06:21,096 --> 00:06:25,178
So a human only ever
approves a real, well-formed action.

105
00:06:25,528 --> 00:06:30,944
Asking about nonsense wastes their
attention, and teaches them to click yes without

106
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:31,466
reading.

107
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:34,074
Recap.

108
00:06:34,424 --> 00:06:38,633
Least privilege: only the
tools and powers the job needs.

109
00:06:39,233 --> 00:06:44,850
Read is always allowed, write unless
read-only, and danger needs a human yes.

110
00:06:45,450 --> 00:06:48,719
Validate first, then
ask, with a clear question.

111
00:06:49,319 --> 00:06:52,703
And enforce it in code,
never only in the prompt.

112
00:06:53,303 --> 00:06:55,816
You now know every idea in this module.

113
00:06:56,166 --> 00:07:01,495
Next lesson, we put them together,
and build the personal productivity agent.
